  <div class="eyebrow">Compendium · kit 1.0.3 · November 1, 2026 · Marco Giribaldi</div>
  <h1>Endosymbiosis 1.0.3</h1>
  <p class="lede">Endosymbiosis takes the rules of Symbiosis that, written down, weren't enough, and turns them into programs that look after them. The last word is still yours.</p>
  <div class="descargas"><a href="#instalar">How to install it</a><a href="../symbiosis/">Read Symbiosis</a></div>
  <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
    <a href="#trata">What it's about</a><a href="#depende">Why it depends on Symbiosis</a><a href="#piezas">The pieces</a><a href="#probado">Where it was tested</a><a href="#parches">Upcoming patches</a><a href="#instalar">How to install it</a><a href="#referencias">References</a>
  </nav>
</header>

<section id="trata">
  <div class="eyebrow">1</div>
  <h2>What it's about</h2>
  <p>Symbiosis is a pact between a person and their AI: a shared memory, an agreement that binds both of them, and one clear principle: the user always has the last word. That pact is a contract, a contract made of habits. But habits fail: in a rush, the AI can overlook what is written, and when the model changes or the system grows, what is written gets forgotten.</p>
  <p>Endosymbiosis turns that contract into structure. When a written rule fails, an <strong>organ</strong> is born: a small program that looks after that rule. The organ becomes part of the system, is checked at every startup, and reports on how it is working. The organs live in a folder protected by a guardian: the AI reads them and runs them; changing them is up to you (and with the guardian in code, the AI can't change them anyway). Both the AI and the user can propose changes, but only the user authorizes them. The guardian comes in two modes: in prose, its rules are written down and the AI follows them; in code, it also stops the AI. You start in prose. The system does five different jobs:</p>
  <div class="two">
    <div class="box"><h3>It prevents the mistake before it happens</h3><p>With the guardian in code, before the AI acts, the system checks what it is about to do. If it tries to delete a file or enter a private folder, the system stops it and explains why. And in both modes, a filter checks every save so that a password or a personal document doesn't get into the history. When it stops a deletion, it tells the AI what to do instead; for example, move the file to a predefined discard folder that only the user can delete.</p></div>
    <div class="box"><h3>The same rules for every AI in the system</h3><p>With the guardian in code, if a single model is working, it stops that model. If several are working, for example one that does the work and another that reviews it, it stops all of them equally, as long as they work from the same environment. They can use different programs: the written rules are the same for all of them, and the brake in code reaches the programs the guardian is connected to. None of them can modify the folder where the organs live, and turning off the guardian is the user's decision.</p></div>
    <div class="box"><h3>The last word belongs to the user</h3><p>With the guardian in code, permission stops being a “yes” in the conversation, which can be misread, and becomes temporary access that only the user opens with their password. The AI can propose changes to the organs, but only the user can apply them, just like installing or removing the system. And if the AI needs to work in a private folder, it is the user who opens the way for a few minutes; after that, the permission expires on its own.</p></div>
    <div class="box"><h3>It detects omissions and contradictions in memory</h3><p>At the start of each session, it compares the list of pending items with its history and, if one has disappeared without being closed or postponed, it warns you and copies what it said. It also points out specific contradictions, such as something closed that is still listed as pending, or an overdue date that isn't flagged. Before claiming that something doesn't exist, it examines the current notes and their whole history, and shows where it looked. It never fixes anything on its own: it warns, and the user decides.</p></div>
    <div class="box"><h3>It keeps a record of who did what</h3><p>Every change saved to the history carries a signature: which AI wrote it or, if the user did it alone, their mark. Separately, the user's decisions are written down in the logbook in their exact words. At the end of the session, an organ checks the signatures and warns in red if a change was left unsigned: the session doesn't close until the change has an owner. With that data, it prepares a draft AI-use disclosure statement.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p>Memory is built the way Symbiosis teaches. Endosymbiosis doesn't replace it or write it: it watches over it from within.</p>
  <p>The name comes from biology. In endosymbiosis, one organism comes to live inside another and becomes part of it, like the mitochondrion in the cell: it produces the energy, but the nucleus stays in command. The same thing happens here: the organs do the work, but they don't give the orders.</p>
  <div class="two">
    <div class="box"><h3>What it is</h3><ul class="plain"><li>A way of working: every rule starts out written and, if it fails, moves to code.</li><li>Six organs born from real failures, installed in about five minutes with Python and git.</li><li>A single tool so that human and AI work as one system.</li></ul></div>
    <div class="box"><h3>What it isn't</h3><ul class="plain"><li>It isn't all code: whatever prose looks after well stays in prose.</li><li>It isn't infallible: it stops the mistakes it already knows, and every new mistake helps improve it.</li><li>It isn't a finished product: it's a research version that its author uses every day.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="depende">
  <div class="eyebrow">2</div>
  <h2>Why it depends on Symbiosis</h2>
  <p>Symbiosis is the foundation. It teaches you to build a core of notes where the rules of the agreement live: they are followed by habit, without programs, and they work with any AI. Endosymbiosis starts from there. It doesn't invent new rules; it takes the ones Symbiosis already wrote down (set aside instead of deleting, don't store passwords, let nothing disappear in silence) and looks after them with code. Without those notes, there would be nothing to look after.</p>
  <p>Symbiosis itself marks the moment to take that step:</p>
  <p class="prosa" style="margin:0">If a rule is broken twice, or prose is no longer enough, write down in the status which rule it was and how it failed, and tell the person: turning it into code is the next step, and it only happens when they ask for it.<cite>Symbiosis 2.1, Guide for the Machine</cite></p>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Symbiosis</th><th>Endosymbiosis</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Role</strong></td><td>The foundation: sets the rules and builds the memory</td><td>The reinforcement: looks after those rules with code</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>How it's enforced</strong></td><td>By hand, through habits</td><td>With organs that watch over the rules and are tested at every startup</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Which AI</strong></td><td>Any, from a chat</td><td>One that works directly with your files</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Notes</strong></td><td>Six notes and a home note</td><td>The same ones, plus a <a class="ir" href="#registro">logbook</a>: each decision or mistake on one line, with the date and the words of whoever decided</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>When</strong></td><td>From day one</td><td>When a rule fails twice or prose is no longer enough</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Endosymbiosis builds on Symbiosis 2.1. With earlier versions, some habits clash with the organs.</p>
</section>

<section id="piezas">
  <div class="eyebrow">3</div>
  <h2>The pieces, from prose to code</h2>
  <p>Each piece is presented the same way: what it's for, the written rule it was born from, what used to happen, what it does today, how you know it works, and its limits. The limits matter as much as the rest: a protection that keeps quiet about them promises more than it delivers.</p>
  <p>Each card opens with a sentence from Symbiosis: the rule that, written down, wasn't enough. The organ is that same rule, now looked after by a program. When the rule is meant for the AI, the sentence comes from the guide that Symbiosis writes for it.</p>
  <p>A word you'll see often: the history. It's what git keeps every time you make a save (a “commit”): whatever wasn't saved isn't there. No piece is born finished: each one evolves with use. At the end of this section is the guardian's story.</p>
  <p>Not all of them are programs: the cage is a lock; the pre-flight checklist, a list the canary runs; and the logbook, a note. And the guardian comes in prose and moves to code when prose isn't enough.</p>

  <h3 class="grupo">I · The ones that hold up the system</h3>
  <article class="piece" id="guardian">
    <header><span class="kind">Stops it before it happens</span><h3>The guardian</h3>
      <p class="para"><strong>What it's for:</strong> so the AI doesn't delete or touch what it shouldn't, even when it's in a hurry.</p></header>
    <p class="prosa">The grading isn't paranoia. It's a seat belt: it lets you go fast because you're buckled in.<cite>Symbiosis 2.1, Step IV, The Three Zones</cite></p>
    <div class="arrow">↓ becomes</div>
    <div class="cmd">organos/guardian.py   · prose by default; code with: python3 instalar.py --guardian si</div>
    <dl>
      <dt>Before</dt><dd>When it was only a written rule, the AI knew it and still, in a hurry, skipped it: it deleted a file to “clean up”, or read a private folder looking for something. You only noticed afterwards.</dd>
      <dt>What it does</dt><dd><p>It comes in two modes.</p>
        <p><strong>In prose</strong>, the starting mode: the rules are written in the agreement and the AI follows them. It doesn't stop anything. At the start of each session, the <a class="ir" href="#canario">canary</a> checks whether anything was deleted or the organs changed, and if it finds something, it recommends switching to code.</p>
        <p><strong>In code</strong>, the guardian checks every action of the AI before it happens and stops four things:</p>
        <ul><li>deleting files or git history;</li><li>entering the private folders on your list;</li><li>changing the organs (reading them is allowed);</li><li>getting around the <a class="ir" href="#secretos">secrets filter</a>.</li></ul>
        <p>Everything else goes through, except a command too long to check in time: it stops that too, and asks you to put it in a file. When it stops something, it explains why and what to do instead. For example, if the AI wants to delete a file, it tells it to set the file aside: to move it to a discard folder, where it stays safe until you decide to empty it. The card <a href="#apartar">Set aside instead of deleting</a> explains how.</p>
        <p>Switching modes is up to you, from your terminal: today it's a rule the AI follows; in the next version it will also ask for a confirmation word (see Upcoming patches).</p></dd>
      <dt>Inside</dt><dd>It receives the command or the file the AI wants to use. It breaks the command into parts and leaves out whatever is only text (a message, a note). With what's left, it decides “go ahead” or “stopped”, with the reason, almost instantly; and if something fails inside it, it stops.</dd>
      <dt>How you know it works</dt><dd>Every time you start a session, the canary puts it to the test: it asks it to do forbidden things, like deleting a test file. If the guardian stops them, you see «guardián vivo» (guardian alive). If any gets through, you see «guardián desconectado» (guardian disconnected) in red. In prose there's nothing to test: the canary reminds you that the guardian is in prose.</dd>
      <dt class="lim">Its limits</dt><dd>It reads the text of the command, not what a program does inside: a hidden deletion can get through. It's a layer, not a wall; the wall is git and the system's backups, which bring back what was lost. With an AI that doesn't consult it, it stops nothing. And sometimes it stops too much: if the AI names a private folder in a command, even just mentioning it, it stops the command; when that happens, it tells you and you take it from there. And it will never close every workaround: it covers the basics, and whatever slips through, the wall brings back.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>

  <article class="piece" id="jaula">
    <header><span class="kind">Protects the organs</span><h3>The cage</h3>
      <p class="para"><strong>What it's for:</strong> so the AI can use the organs, but never change them.</p></header>
    <p class="prosa">The machine verifies, when you give it something to check against; it names and proposes, at low cost, within the medium it lives in. You decide, judge, and execute, and you answer for the consequences in the real world.<cite>Symbiosis 2.1, Step III, Six Verbs, Not Two</cite></p>
    <div class="arrow">↓ becomes</div>
    <div class="cmd">organos/   with your computer's lock
python3 instalar.py --abrir    (opens it)
python3 instalar.py --cerrar   (locks it again)</div>
    <dl>
      <dt>Before</dt><dd>The organs were programs like any other, and the AI could change them. A rule that the one being watched can rewrite watches nothing: it's like asking a student to grade their own exam.</dd>
      <dt>What it does</dt><dd>The organs live in a locked folder: the cage. The AI can read them and use them, but not change them. If an organ needs a change, the AI proposes it and you install it: you open the cage from your terminal, apply the change, and lock it again. With the <a class="ir" href="#guardian">guardian</a> in prose there's no lock: the rule is written down, and at the start of each session the <a class="ir" href="#canario">canary</a> checks that the organs haven't changed.</dd>
      <dt>Inside</dt><dd>The lock isn't something the program invents: it's the same safeguard your computer uses to protect its own files. And on top of that, the guardian stops the AI if it tries to write there.</dd>
      <dt>How you know it works</dt><dd>At the start of each session, the canary looks at the cage. If the lock is on, it says so: «jaula con candado» (cage locked). If someone removed it, it warns you in yellow so you can put it back.</dd>
      <dt class="lim">Its limits</dt><dd>On Linux the lock is solid: it can only be removed with the administrator's password. On Mac and Windows it's weaker, like the latch on a door: the same account can remove it, and the canary only warns you at the start of the next session. And in prose there's no lock: the canary compares the organs against a fingerprint that, for now, the AI could rewrite (see Upcoming patches).</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>

  <article class="piece" id="canario">
    <header><span class="kind">Takes roll call at the start</span><h3>The canary</h3>
      <p class="para"><strong>What it's for:</strong> so you find out, before you start working, whether anything was lost, whether anything doesn't add up, and whether all the organs are still online.</p></header>
    <p class="prosa">If something is missing, something was lost; if something is extra, something is lying.<cite>Symbiosis 2.1, Step II, The Memory Canary</cite></p>
    <div class="arrow">↓ becomes</div>
    <div class="cmd">python3 organos/canario.py</div>
    <dl>
      <dt>Before</dt><dd>It was a written rule: at the start of each session, take roll call by hand, like a pilot before takeoff. But by hand it got skipped, or rushed, and mistakes slipped through. Once, while making a clean copy of the pending list, some tasks were lost and nobody noticed in time: that's forgetting. And another time, a note said a program was still running when we had already retired it: that's lying.</dd>
      <dt>What it does</dt><dd><p>Now a program takes roll call on its own, every time you start. It looks for two things:</p>
        <ul><li><strong>what was lost:</strong> a pending item that was once written down and isn't there today, without anyone having closed it. It tells you which one, not just that one is missing;</li><li><strong>what is lying:</strong> a task crossed off on an earlier day, something marked as closed without proof, a duplicated pending item, an overdue date with no warning.</li></ul>
        <p><strong>It also keeps an eye on the others: that the <a class="ir" href="#guardian">guardian</a> responds, that the <a class="ir" href="#secretos">secrets filter</a> is in place, that the <a class="ir" href="#jaula">cage</a> has its lock, and that there's a recent copy in the history. It shows you all this in green, yellow, or red.</strong></p>
        <p>With the guardian in prose, it also checks that the organs haven't changed and that nothing was deleted without going through the <a class="ir" href="#apartar">discard folder</a>.</p></dd>
      <dt>Inside</dt><dd>It compares your list of pending items with everything that was ever saved in the history. Whatever was written down and disappeared without being closed, it flags. Then it checks each organ in its own way. For the guardian, it sets small traps, asking it to do forbidden things, to make sure it really stops them. For the secrets filter, that it's still hooked into the history. For the cage, that it has its lock. And for the history, when the last copy was saved.</dd>
      <dt>How you know it works</dt><dd>Its test hides failures on purpose, like a lost pending item or an old crossed-off task, and requires it to find all of them. If it misses one, the test comes out red.</dd>
      <dt class="lim">Its limits</dt><dd>It warns, but never fixes: whatever it finds, you decide. It only sees what is written: it doesn't know whether something is really done; you know that. With the guardian in prose it sees carelessness, but not tricks: an AI that wanted to hide something could fool it. And one odd case: if you move the folder somewhere else without reinstalling, the canary keeps looking for the guardian at the old address, doesn't find it, and can still show you green. If you're going to move the folder, uninstall first, then install in the new location.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>

  <article class="piece" id="chequeos">
    <header><span class="kind">Part of the canary · checks what's specific to your project</span><h3>The pre-flight checklist</h3>
      <p class="para"><strong>What it's for:</strong> to check what can't fail in your project before you start working. If the cockpit is in order, the canary can fly.</p></header>
    <p class="prosa">A mistake you don't remember repeats itself; a mistake you remember becomes law.<cite>Symbiosis 2.1, Step II, Memory as an Immune System</cite></p>
    <div class="arrow">↓ becomes</div>
    <div class="cmd">organos/chequeos.txt   · one line per check:
name | command | what it should answer</div>
    <dl>
      <dt>Before</dt><dd>Every project has its usual stumbles: an important file that got emptied by accident, a service that stopped responding, a step that always gets forgotten. Each mistake was written down as a rule on the canary's list, to be checked at the start. But the check was done by hand, and when it got skipped, the mistake happened again.</dd>
      <dt>What it does</dt><dd>Now that list is no longer checked by hand. Each recorded mistake becomes a question the <a class="ir" href="#canario">canary</a> asks on its own, every time you start: “is the important file still full?”, “is the service responding?”. If an answer isn't what it should be, it warns you in red before you start working. And when a new mistake repeats, the AI suggests adding it to the list, and you decide.</dd>
      <dt>Inside</dt><dd>Each recorded mistake is a line with three parts: its name, the command that checks it, and the correct answer. The canary runs the command and compares. If the answer takes more than ten seconds, or isn't the correct one, it comes out red.</dd>
      <dt>How you know it works</dt><dd>At startup you see how many questions passed, for example “3/3”. If a line is badly written, it also comes out red, so that a badly recorded mistake doesn't pass for watched.</dd>
      <dt class="lim">Its limits</dt><dd>It comes empty: you fill it, mistake by mistake, as they show up. It only watches what you wrote down: a new mistake gets through the first time. Writing the command for each question takes some technical skill, but the AI puts it together for you. Nobody but the author has used it yet.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>

  <h3 class="grupo">II · The ones that record the decisions</h3>
  <article class="piece" id="registro">
    <header><span class="kind">A note, not a program · keeps the why</span><h3>The logbook</h3>
      <p class="para"><strong>What it's for:</strong> to remember why things are the way they are, in the words of whoever decided.</p></header>
    <p class="prosa">It isn't yours or the model's, but both of yours: a shared notebook where the decisions, the findings, and the mistakes turned into law all live.<cite>Symbiosis 2.1, Step II, The Third Memory</cite></p>
    <div class="arrow">↓ becomes</div>
    <div class="cmd">nucleo/decisions.md   · one line per decision or stumble:
date · what happened · your words · what changed</div>
    <dl>
      <dt>Before</dt><dd>Stumbles ended up mixed in with the pending items, and got lost. And each new mistake became one more rule in the agreement, the note with the working rules you agree on with your AI. It grew to more than a hundred, and nobody could follow them all anymore.</dd>
      <dt>What it does</dt><dd>It isn't a program: it's one more note in the core. Each decision or stumble leaves a line, with the date and your exact words. And it has a simple rule: a mistake doesn't create a new rule, it creates a line. If it repeats, it moves to the <a class="ir" href="#chequeos">pre-flight checklist</a>. If even that isn't enough, it becomes an organ. That's how Endosymbiosis grows, one step at a time.</dd>
      <dt>How you know it works</dt><dd>It works when the system itself can tell you why a rule or an organ exists: the answer is in the logbook, with its date and your words. It also remembers its own mistakes and failures, so they don't repeat. And it keeps the agreement from filling up with rules: if it grows too much, the <a class="ir" href="#canario">canary</a> warns you, because an AI with too many rules can no longer follow them all.</dd>
      <dt class="lim">Its limits</dt><dd>It's a note, not a program: it only keeps what someone writes. If a decision was made and nobody wrote it down, here it doesn't exist. And no program checks that every one gets written down: it depends on habit, the AI's and yours.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>

  <article class="piece" id="daisy">
    <header><span class="kind">Says who did what</span><h3>The signature on every change (DAISY)</h3>
      <p class="para"><strong>What it's for:</strong> so there's a written record of who made each change and who answers for it: what the AI did and what you decided.</p></header>
    <p class="prosa">This book was written in collaboration with artificial intelligence models, which is exactly what it's about. The decisions, the experiences, and the mistakes are mine.<cite>Symbiosis 2.1, Credits</cite></p>
    <div class="arrow">↓ becomes</div>
    <div class="cmd">python3 organos/daisy.py --declaracion</div>
    <dl>
      <dt>Before</dt><dd>It was a sentence at the end of the work, like the one above: “it was made with AI; the decisions are mine”. It's honest, but nobody can check it. In Symbiosis the two of you work together, but there was no honest way to know where you acted and where the AI did.</dd>
      <dt>What it does</dt><dd>Like the signature at the bottom of a document, every change that gets saved carries the name of whoever made it: the AI signs with its model's name, and what you do by hand carries your mark. When a change comes from a decision of yours, the AI copies it in your exact words. At the close of each session, the program counts the signatures: how many changes the AI made, how many cite your decision, how many another model reviewed, and how many you made. <strong>It doesn't measure how much you thought: it shows where your decision was written down.</strong> If a change was left unsigned, it marks it in red, and the session doesn't close until you give it an owner. And if you ask, it writes a draft AI-use disclosure statement in the DAISY format, in English because it's meant for journals (see <a href="#referencias">References</a>).</dd>
      <dt>Inside</dt><dd>It reads the changes in the history since the last close and looks for each one's signature: the model's name, your mark, or your words quoted next to your name. If they all have an owner, or if there were no changes, it shows green. If one is missing, it stops. To give a change an owner you don't need to touch the past: a new, signed change that explains it is enough.</dd>
      <dt>How you know it works</dt><dd>At the close of each session you see the count: how many changes there were, what share the AI signed, how many cite your decision, how many another model reviewed, and how many you made. And its test builds a practice history with an unsigned change and requires it to stop.</dd>
      <dt class="lim">Its limits</dt><dd>It sees who wrote each change and where your decision was quoted. It doesn't see how much you thought beforehand, or what was said outside the history, like a chat in another window. If a decision of yours wasn't quoted, for the program it doesn't exist. That's why your judgment is recorded where you stepped in, in your words, and not as a percentage of authorship. That's what we propose adding to DAISY.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>

  <h3 class="grupo">III · The everyday tools</h3>
  <article class="piece" id="apartar">
    <header><span class="kind">The path that replaces deleting</span><h3>Set aside instead of deleting</h3>
      <p class="para"><strong>What it's for:</strong> so nothing gets lost in a cleanup: whatever's left over is kept aside, and only you empty it.</p></header>
    <p class="prosa">Deleting stops being a single step with no return and becomes two: setting aside, which can be undone, and emptying, which is yours.<cite>Symbiosis 2.1, Step IV, The Three Zones</cite></p>
    <div class="arrow">↓ becomes</div>
    <div class="cmd">python3 organos/apartar.py &lt;file&gt; &lt;reason&gt;</div>
    <dl>
      <dt>Before</dt><dd>It was a written rule: don't delete, set aside. But in a hurry, the AI sometimes deleted anyway, or “moved” a file by copying it and deleting the original. In the project's history, that showed up as a deletion.</dd>
      <dt>What it does</dt><dd>Now there's only one way to remove something: set it aside. A program moves whatever's left over to a discard folder, inside a box labeled with the date and the reason, and tells you where it went. Nothing is deleted: the file is still there, with its entire history. You review that folder and empty it when you're sure. And if the AI tries to delete something, the <a class="ir" href="#guardian">guardian</a> stops it and suggests setting it aside: that way it always has a way out that loses nothing.</dd>
      <dt>Inside</dt><dd>It works like a move with labeled boxes: it looks for the nearest discard folder, makes a box inside it with the date and the reason, and moves the file there. If the history knows the file, it moves it through the history, so it's recorded as a move and not as a deletion, and it reminds you to save that move as a separate change, without mixing it with other work.</dd>
      <dt>How you know it works</dt><dd>At the start of each session, the <a class="ir" href="#canario">canary</a> looks at the discard folder. If it holds things more than a month old, or it's getting heavy, it reminds you in yellow to review it. And with the guardian in prose, it also warns you if something disappeared without going through it.</dd>
      <dt class="lim">Its limits</dt><dd>It doesn't empty anything: emptying is always yours. It doesn't set aside the organs' folder. And it only looks after what goes through it: if something is deleted some other way, setting aside doesn't see it; that's what the guardian and the history are for.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>

  <article class="piece" id="secretos">
    <header><span class="kind">Part of the guardian · watches what goes into the history</span><h3>The secrets filter</h3>
      <p class="para"><strong>What it's for:</strong> to protect you from leaking an important piece of data. A password or an ID number that gets into the project's history stays there, and anyone who receives a copy can see it.</p></header>
    <p class="prosa">What can't be there: a password or private data.<cite>Symbiosis 2.1, Step II, The Memory Canary</cite></p>
    <div class="arrow">↓ becomes</div>
    <div class="cmd">organos/secretos.py   · checks every save in git; works in both of the guardian's modes</div>
    <dl>
      <dt>Before</dt><dd>It was a written rule: don't store passwords or private data. The AI knew it, but sometimes it slipped. And the danger is real: if an access key (the key your project uses to access a service, such as a paid AI) gets into the history and the project is shared or made public, anyone can use it in your name, and the charges come to you. Whatever gets into the history can't be deleted anymore. It happened to us with something minor: a test ID number got saved twice.</dd>
      <dt>What it does</dt><dd>Now a program checks every change before it's saved and flags possible sensitive data: a password, an access key, or an ID number. When it sees one, it stops the save and tells you where it is, without showing the data. You weigh the risk and decide: if it's real, you take it out; if it's a false alarm, you save it by hand from your terminal. Your judgment always comes first; the program keeps something important from slipping past you through carelessness.</dd>
      <dt>Inside</dt><dd>It doesn't understand what's written: it looks for shapes that repeat, the way you'd recognize a fingerprint. Many access keys start the same way (GitHub's, for example, with “ghp_”). A password usually comes after the word “password”. A DNI (a national ID number) is eight digits next to the word DNI. If the new text has one of those shapes, it warns you.</dd>
      <dt>How you know it works</dt><dd>Like a warning light on a car's dashboard: if everything's fine, you don't see it; if something fails, it lights up. Every time you start working, the <a class="ir" href="#canario">canary</a> checks that the filter is in place. If it's missing, it warns you in red.</dd>
      <dt class="lim">Its limits</dt><dd>It only recognizes what looks like a secret: a loose piece of data, or one inside a photo or a PDF, can get through. The final check is yours.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>

  <article class="piece" id="libre">
    <header><span class="kind">The permission you give</span><h3>Temporary access</h3>
      <p class="para"><strong>What it's for:</strong> so the AI works in your private folders only when you decide, and only for a while.</p></header>
    <p class="prosa">If it asks your permission even to read, you get used to saying “yes” without looking, and that automatic “yes” will slip out of you just when it matters.<cite>Symbiosis 2.1, Step IV, The Three Zones</cite></p>
    <div class="arrow">↓ becomes</div>
    <div class="cmd">python3 organos/libre.py 30    (opens for 30 minutes)
python3 organos/libre.py fin   (closes early)       · only with the guardian in code</div>
    <dl>
      <dt>Before</dt><dd>It was a written rule: the AI doesn't enter your private folders without your permission. But the permission was a word in the chat, and a word gets said on autopilot, gets misread, or the AI assumes it was said.</dd>
      <dt>What it does</dt><dd>When you want the AI to work in your private folders, you open them yourself, from your terminal and with your computer's password (on Windows, by opening the terminal as administrator), for as long as you choose: half an hour, an afternoon, eight hours at most. When the time is up, they close on their own; you can also close them earlier. The AI can't open them by itself.</dd>
      <dt>Inside</dt><dd>It writes down the time until which access is open, in a place on the computer where only the administrator can write. Before every action, the <a class="ir" href="#guardian">guardian</a> reads that time. If the entry could have been written by the AI, it doesn't accept it.</dd>
      <dt>How you know it works</dt><dd>If you type the command without a time, it tells you whether access is open and until when. At the start of each session, the <a class="ir" href="#canario">canary</a> also notes it. And when it expires, the guardian goes back to stopping the AI in your private folders.</dd>
      <dt class="lim">Its limits</dt><dd>It opens your private folders and nothing else: even while access is open, the AI still can't delete anything or change the organs. It only works if the guardian is in code. And it comes with one condition: never run the AI as the computer's administrator, because then it could grant itself access. On Windows it hasn't been tested in real use yet.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>

  <article class="piece" id="buscar">
    <header><span class="kind">Searches before saying “it doesn't exist”</span><h3>The search tool</h3>
      <p class="para"><strong>What it's for:</strong> so the AI doesn't tell you “that doesn't exist” without really searching, and shows you where it looked.</p></header>
    <p class="prosa">“I can't find it” is not “it doesn't exist.”<cite>Symbiosis 2.1, Step II, What Works for Us</cite></p>
    <div class="arrow">↓ becomes</div>
    <div class="cmd">python3 organos/buscar.py "irrigation|drip"</div>
    <dl>
      <dt>Before</dt><dd>It was a written rule: before saying “it doesn't exist”, search. But the AI only looked at what was in plain sight and said “it doesn't exist”, when the answer was saved in an earlier version in the history. Like someone who looks for their keys only on the table and concludes they're lost.</dd>
      <dt>What it does</dt><dd>Now the AI searches with a program that doesn't stop at the table. It goes through your current files and also the history, where everything that was ever saved remains, even if it was later changed or deleted. When it's done, it tells you whether it found it or not, and in which places it looked. If something is said in several ways, you can search for all of them at once, for example “irrigation” and “drip”.</dd>
      <dt>Inside</dt><dd>It goes through today's text files. Then it asks the history for every change where that word appeared or disappeared. It combines the two lists and ends with a fixed line, with the count, which the AI copies without making anything up.</dd>
      <dt>How you know it works</dt><dd>Its test hides a word in an old version in the history and requires it to find it.</dd>
      <dt class="lim">Its limits</dt><dd>It searches for words, not ideas: if something was said another way, you have to give it the synonym. An accent mark counts: “café” and “cafe” are two different searches. It doesn't see what was never saved. And it reads everything it's asked to: never hand it a private folder. In a test with our own project, the AI had to read between 14 and 481 times less text than when searching by hand (three searches, estimated), but it also showed us something to improve: some very long lines in the history still weigh too much. That's noted for the next version.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>

  <div class="box" id="crece"><h3>How an organ grows</h3>
    <p>No lab test finds what real use finds. Each organ keeps a record of what it did, and that record shows where it gets in the way or where it fails. Then it gets corrected, without losing the basics, and goes back into use.</p>
    <p>The guardian is the example. In its first eight days of use it stopped the AI 55 times; when we reviewed the record, only 3 of those stops had protected anything. The rest was friction: it confused reading the organs' folder with writing to it, or a text that merely said “delete” with an actual deletion. And two users with no programming background had trouble using it with several models. That's where version 1.0.3 came from, with a guardian that stops things needlessly less often, and its prose mode. An auditor who hadn't written the code attacked it over five rounds, until the basics were covered without anything being stopped needlessly. And the first test on Windows found a bug that no audit had seen.</p>
    <p>A simple guardian is more powerful: it looks after only what matters, and that's why nobody turns it off. That's how Endosymbiosis grows: a written rule that fails moves to code, and code that gets in the way is adjusted again through use.</p>
  </div>
</section>

